[38;2;124;124;124mSo the approach of showing d has a prime factor not in n is the right one. Let [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mme think about how to prove this for all composite n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me look at the structure more carefully. Let me consider the "reflected" [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msum.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124md = Σ_{i=1}^{k-1} dᵢd_{i+1}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mUsing the pairing dᵢ ↔ d_{k+1-i} (with dᵢ · d_{k+1-i} = n):[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor k even (n not a perfect square):[0m
[38;2;124;124;124md = Σ_{i=1}^{k/2-1} (dᵢd_{i+1} + d_{k-i}d_{k+1-i}) + d_{k/2}d_{k/2+1}[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mwhere d_{k/2}d_{k/2+1} = n (the middle pair, since d_{k/2}·d_{k/2+1} = n as [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(k/2)+(k/2+1) = k+1).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAnd dᵢd_{i+1} + d_{k-i}d_{k+1-i} = xᵢ + n²/xᵢ where xᵢ = dᵢd_{i+1}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor k odd (n is a perfect square):[0m
[38;2;124;124;124md = Σ_{i=1}^{(k-1)/2} (dᵢd_{i+1} + d_{k-i}d_{k+1-i})[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mwith no middle term (the middle divisor is √n, and it pairs with itself).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually wait, for k odd, the terms are i=1,...,k-1, and we pair i with k-i. The[0m[38;2;124;124;124mmiddle term is when i = k-i, i.e., i = k/2, but k is odd so there's no such [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minteger. So all terms pair up. The pairs are (i, k-i) for i = 1, ..., (k-1)/2.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor k even, the middle term is i = k/2: d_{k/2}·d_{k/2+1} = n. The pairs are (i,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mk-i) for i = 1, ..., k/2-1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- k even: d = n + Σ_{i=1}^{k/2-1} (xᵢ + n²/xᵢ)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- k odd: d = Σ_{i=1}^{(k-1)/2} (xᵢ + n²/xᵢ)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mwhere xᵢ = dᵢd_{i+1} for the "lower half" indices.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, xᵢ + n²/xᵢ = (xᵢ² + n²)/xᵢ. Since xᵢ | n² (as dᵢ | n and d_{i+1} | n), we [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhave n²/xᵢ is an integer. So each pair sum is an integer.[0m

